    "description": "# Future Directions: Topological Quantum Codes from Homology\n\nThis cycle established, as exact theorems, that the number of logical qubits of a\nCSS code equals the first Betti number of its underlying complex, that the\ngenus-`g` surface encodes exactly `2g` logical qubits, and that code distance is\nthe homological systole. The following conjectures extend those findings.\n\n## Conjecture 1 \u2014 Homological packing bound\n\nFor every CSS code arising from a `2`-complex embeddable in a closed surface,\nthe parameters satisfy `k \u00b7 d\u00b2 \u2264 c \u00b7 n` for an absolute constant `c`, with\nequality approached by regular toric layouts.\n\nThe key insight is that the systole and the co-systole cannot both be large in a\nfixed number of cells: a short nontrivial cycle in one direction is forced by a\nshort cut in the dual, so distance and rate trade off quadratically rather than\nindependently.\n\nWhy now? The systolic definition of distance and the exact dimension law proved\nthis cycle make the two sides of the inequality directly comparable in a single\nhomological framework, so the bound can be attacked cell-by-cell rather than\nthrough analytic geometry.\n\n## Conjecture 2 \u2014 Genus is the sole obstruction to rate\n\nTwo closed orientable surfaces give CSS codes of equal logical dimension if and\nonly if they have equal genus; no finer cellulation data affects `k`.\n\nThe key insight is that `k = dim H\u2081` is a homotopy invariant, so it depends only\non the topological type of the surface and is blind to the combinatorics of the\ntessellation, which instead controls `n` and `d`.\n\nWhy now? Having reduced `k` to a Betti number that we compute exactly for the\nminimal complex, the invariance claim becomes a statement purely about `H\u2081`,\ndetached from any particular incidence matrix.\n\n## Conjecture 3 \u2014 Hyperbolic surfaces beat Euclidean ones\n\nCodes from negatively curved (genus `\u2265 2`) surface tessellations achieve constant\nrate `k/n` bounded away from zero while retaining distance growing like `log n`,\nstrictly outperforming the vanishing rate of flat toric codes.\n\nThe key insight is that hyperbolic area grows with boundary length, so a fixed\nfraction of cells can be handles: many independent homology classes coexist\nwithout shrinking the systole as fast as in the Euclidean case.\n\nWhy now? The framework already separates the three parameters `n`, `k`, `d`\nhomologically, so curvature enters only through how fast the systole grows with\ncell count \u2014 a single scalar to estimate rather than a full geometric analysis.\n\n## Conjecture 4 \u2014 Color codes as quotient complexes\n\nEvery color code on a `3`-valent, `3`-colorable `2`-complex is equivalent to a\nsurface code on an explicit branched double cover, with matching distance.\n\nThe key insight is that the color-code stabilizer structure is the homology of a\ncomplex with extra face-coloring, and folding the colors realises it as an\nordinary chain complex whose systole is preserved by the cover.\n\nWhy now? With CSS codes uniformly presented as chain complexes and distance as a\nsystole, the color-code-to-surface-code dictionary becomes a comparison of two\ncomplexes rather than an ad hoc stabilizer manipulation.\n",
