    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Universal Mathematics\n\nThis mission formalizes a semantic notion of **universal mathematics**: the\ntheorems any sufficiently expressive, consistent reasoner must accept, modeled as\nsentences entailed by every consistent extension of a base theory. The Lean\ndevelopment lives in:\n\n* `Catalog/Logic/UniversalMathematics.lean` \u2014 the framework and metatheorems.\n* `Catalog/Logic/AlienArithmetic.lean` \u2014 primes as a definitional invariant.\n\n## What is proved (0 sorries, only standard axioms)\n\n* **Peano-style universality = monotonicity.** `entails_mono`: every theorem of a\n  theory survives in every extension; `universal_iff_entails`: over a consistent\n  base theory, *universal = provable*. This is the exact content of \"a base\n  theory's theorems are a subset of the theorems of every consistent extension\".\n* **The parallel postulate is not universal.** `independent_not_universal`:\n  a sentence with both a model and a countermodel is not universal, and neither is\n  its negation. Instantiated by `commutativity_not_universal` (abelian vs.\n  non-abelian groups), the algebraic mirror of Euclidean vs. non-Euclidean\n  geometry \u2014 while `commutativity_universal_over_abelian` shows the same sentence\n  becomes universal once adopted as an axiom.\n* **The Riemann-Hypothesis reduction.** `universal_or_iff_decided`: over a\n  consistent theory, \"\u03c6 or \u00ac\u03c6 is universal\" is equivalent to \"the theory decides\n  \u03c6\". Read arithmetically, \"RH is universal\" \u21d4 \"arithmetic decides RH\" \u2014 an open\n  problem, so RH universality is left as a genuine conjecture, never asserted.\n* **Aliens discover primes.** `prime_iff_divisibility`, `prime_iff_indecomposable`,\n  `prime_iff_algebraic`: primes are pinned down by the divisibility order, by\n  multiplicative indecomposability, and by the abstract `Prime`/`Irreducible`\n  notions valid in any commutative monoid. `minFac_is_prime` gives the canonical\n  prime-finder, and `infinitude_of_primes` + `factorization_exists`/`_unique`\n  give Euclid's theorem and the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic.\n\n## Natural next steps\n\n1. **Syntactic side and soundness/completeness.** The current model is purely\n   semantic (`\u22a8`). Add a Hilbert- or sequent-style provability relation `\u22a2` and\n   prove soundness (`\u22a2 \u2192 \u22a8`) and, for propositional or first-order fragments,\n   completeness (`\u22a8 \u2192 \u22a2`), tying \"universal\" to genuine derivability.\n\n2. **G\u00f6del incompleteness as a universality obstruction.** Formalize (or connect\n   to a formalization of) the first incompleteness theorem to exhibit sentences\n   that are *decided in no recursively axiomatized consistent extension* of\n   arithmetic \u2014 the sharpest possible failure of universality. Mathlib now has\n   substantial first-order logic infrastructure to build on.\n\n3. **A real geometry instance.** Replace the group analogue with an actual\n   incidence/betweenness model of the parallel postulate: exhibit a model where\n   the Euclidean parallel axiom holds and one where it fails, feeding both into\n   `independent_not_universal`.\n\n4. **Lindenbaum and completeness of extensions.** Prove that every consistent\n   theory extends to a complete one, sharpening `universal_or_iff_decided` into a\n   statement about maximal consistent theories deciding every sentence.\n\n5. **The Riemann Hypothesis.** The genuinely open item. Either a proof of RH would\n   make it universal over arithmetic, or an independence result would make it a\n   second \"parallel postulate\". The framework here is agnostic and simply records\n   the equivalence.\n",
