    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 The Cake-Balancing Ratio\n\nThis cycle established the exact algebra of the balancing-ratio functional for a\nsingle circular dissection and proved that the repeated-bisection sequence keeps\nthe long-run ratio `\u03bc_r` below `2` for every window length `r`.  Two structural\nfacts drove everything: aggregation of consecutive pieces never increases the\nimbalance (`\u03bc_r \u2264 \u03bc_1`), and a dissection using only two piece lengths in ratio\n`2` has balancing ratio at most `2`.  The following conjectures push past the\ncrude constant `2`.\n\n## Conjecture A \u2014 The optimal window-1 constant is strictly below 2\n\nFor window length `r = 1`, there is an infinite cutting sequence whose long-run\nratio satisfies `\u03bc_1 < 2`; in fact the infimum of `\u03bc_1` over all sequences is a\nspecific constant in the open interval `(1, 2)`.\n\nThe key insight is that the bisection scheme wastes balance: immediately after a\npower-of-two stage the next insertions create a size-`2` gap that a smarter,\nstaggered insertion order can dilute, because the *order* in which equal-length\npieces are split is a free parameter that the factor-`2` bound ignores entirely.\n\nWhy now? The single-dissection theory is complete (extremal-window sandwiching,\nscale invariance, equipartition optimality), so the remaining freedom is purely\ncombinatorial \u2014 the insertion order \u2014 which is exactly the variable this\nconjecture isolates.\n\n## Conjecture B \u2014 Aggregation strictly improves the constant\n\nThe long-run constant is strictly monotone in the window length on an initial\nrange: for the optimal sequence, `\u03bc_{r+1} < \u03bc_r` until the windows become large\nenough to wrap a constant fraction of the cake.\n\nThe key insight is that overlapping windows share most of their pieces, so a\nsingle oversized piece is amortised across `r` windows; the larger `r` is, the\nsmaller the relative contribution of any one piece, which the finite identity\n`r\u00b7minArc \u2264 window \u2264 r\u00b7maxArc` already hints at but does not yet make strict.\n\nWhy now? The comparison `\u03bc_r \u2264 \u03bc_1` is proved and non-vacuous (two-valued\ndissections give strict inequality for `r \u2265 2`), so quantifying the *gap* is the\nnatural next step rather than a leap.\n\n## Conjecture C \u2014 Low-discrepancy sequences realise the optimum\n\nThe cutting sequence generated by inserting the `n`-th point at the fractional\npart of `n\u00b7\u03b1` for a badly approximable `\u03b1` (e.g. the golden ratio) achieves the\noptimal `\u03bc_1`, and its value is governed by the continued-fraction expansion of\n`\u03b1`.\n\nThe key insight is that the three-gap phenomenon forces such a sequence to use at\nmost three piece lengths at every stage, with the extreme ratio controlled by the\npartial quotients \u2014 tying the balancing constant to the arithmetic of `\u03b1` exactly\nas badly approximable numbers control classical Diophantine ratios.\n\nWhy now? This connects the balancing ratio to the same badly-approximable /\ncontinued-fraction machinery already developed elsewhere in this project for\nLagrange constants, suggesting a shared extremal principle across the two\nfunctionals.\n\n## Conjecture D \u2014 A dual lower bound: no sequence balances perfectly\n\nEvery infinite cutting sequence has `\u03bc_1 > 1`; more strongly, there is a\nuniversal constant `c > 1` with `\u03bc_1 \u2265 c` for all sequences, so perfect long-run\nbalance is impossible.\n\nThe key insight is that inserting one point splits a single arc into two, so a\nnewly created short piece must coexist for many stages with the long pieces\nelsewhere on the circle, forcing a persistent gap that cannot be driven to `1`.\n\nWhy now? The upper side (`\u03bc_1 \u2264 2`) is settled constructively; pinning the\nmatching lower bound is what would turn the interval `(1, 2)` into a genuine\nextremal problem with a determined answer.\n",
