    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Automatic Sequences and the Kernel Criterion\n\nThis cycle established the finite-kernel criterion for automatic sequences as a\nself-contained, characteristic-free foundation: decimations compose by a single\nsemigroup law, the resulting kernel is closed under decimation, and the class of\nautomatic sequences (over a fixed base) is closed under constants, output\ncodings, and pointwise combinations. The Thue\u2013Morse sequence realises the\ncriterion with a kernel of exactly two elements, and its parity form was matched\nterm-by-term to its multiplicative sign form. The directions below are the\nsharpest testable questions these findings raise.\n\n## 1. Exact kernel size equals the minimal automaton's state count\n\n**Conjecture.** For every base `k \u2265 2` and every `k`-automatic sequence, the\nnumber of distinct decimations equals the number of states of the minimal\ndeterministic automaton that generates it; consequently a sequence with a\nkernel of size `m` cannot be generated by any automaton with fewer than `m`\nstates.\n\n*The key insight is* that decimations are not merely *witnesses* of automaticity\nbut are in canonical bijection with the reachable states of the syntactic\nautomaton, so counting decimations is a state-minimisation algorithm in disguise.\n\n*Why now?* The composition law proved this cycle gives decimations the structure\nof a monoid action; state minimisation is exactly the orbit-counting problem for\nthat action, which is now within reach of an elementary argument rather than an\nautomata-theoretic one.\n\n## 2. Product bases: kernel finiteness is multiplicative\n\n**Conjecture.** A sequence is simultaneously `k`-automatic and `\u2113`-automatic for\nmultiplicatively independent `k, \u2113` only when it is eventually periodic, and in\nthat case its `k`-kernel and `\u2113`-kernel have sizes that are both bounded by the\neventual period.\n\n*The key insight is* that the two decimation monoids generate incompatible\nscaling symmetries unless the sequence already has a translation symmetry, so\ndouble-automaticity forces periodicity \u2014 a finite-kernel avatar of Cobham's\ntheorem.\n\n*Why now?* With closure under pointwise operations and a clean finiteness\ncriterion in hand, the difference sequence `n \u21a6 a(n+1) \u2212 a(n)` can be analysed\ninside the same framework, turning Cobham's dichotomy into a statement about the\nintersection of two finite kernels.\n\n## 3. Closure under Cauchy convolution modulo a prime\n\n**Conjecture.** If `a` and `b` are `p`-automatic with values in `\u2124/p`, then the\nrunning-sum sequence and, more strongly, the mod-`p` Cauchy convolution\n`n \u21a6 \u03a3_{i\u2264n} a(i)\u00b7b(n\u2212i)` are again `p`-automatic.\n\n*The key insight is* that mod-`p` convolution interacts with base-`p` decimation\nthrough a carry-free \"digit convolution\" identity, so the convolution's kernel is\na finite image of the product of the two input kernels.\n\n*Why now?* The catalog already isolates the Thue\u2013Morse convolution powers and\ntheir exact parities; combining those explicit parity formulas with the kernel\nclosure results proved here makes the convolution's finite-kernel bound concrete\nand checkable on small cases first.\n\n## 4. A finiteness dichotomy for the value set\n\n**Conjecture.** A sequence has a finite `k`-kernel **iff** both its value set is\nfinite and each of the finitely many \"colour classes\" `{n : a(n) = c}` is a\n`k`-recognisable set of integers; neither condition alone suffices.\n\n*The key insight is* that the finite-alphabet property proved this cycle is only\n*half* of automaticity \u2014 the other half is that each preimage is itself described\nby an automaton, and the two halves are logically independent.\n\n*Why now?* The evaluation-at-zero map from the kernel to the value set, made\nexplicit this cycle, is precisely the bridge that lets one separate \"few values\"\nfrom \"structured preimages\" and test the two conditions independently.\n\n## 5. Transcendence from two-state kernels\n\n**Conjecture.** Any non-eventually-periodic sequence whose `2`-kernel has exactly\ntwo elements generates, over `\u211d`, a transcendental power series; Thue\u2013Morse is\nthe extremal representative of this family.\n\n*The key insight is* that a two-element kernel forces a strict self-similar\nfunctional equation of degree two for the generating function, and such an\nequation is incompatible with algebraicity over `\u211a(x)` unless the sequence is\nperiodic.\n\n*Why now?* Having pinned the Thue\u2013Morse kernel to a precise two-element set, the\nfunctional equation `G(x) = G(x\u00b2)\u00b7(1\u2212x) + \u2026` can be written down exactly, so the\ntranscendence question becomes an explicit algebraic-independence test rather\nthan an asymptotic estimate.\n",
